;;; A rule's concluded `neq` is stored as a fact — **W30**, regression
;;; `assert-neq-w-x` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and a rule concluding `(neq w ?x)` from `(p ?x)`. `w` is
;;; a name of the program (a rule's `:assert` names it), so `(p w)` is a
;;; candidate.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` and `neq` are relations whose facts are fixed by name identity,
;;; computed and never stored, and a rule's conclusion of one is checked: a
;;; true one adds nothing, a false one makes its state a contradiction
;;; (Q-M1e2.26). At root `(neq w a)` holds and adds nothing. At `{(p w)}` the
;;; rule concludes `(neq w w)`, which is false, so that state is dead. R is
;;; {∅}, one state, root's `(p a)` alone.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The conclusion was stored: root held `(neq w a :rule r)` (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), and `{(p w)}` held `(neq w a)` and the false `(neq w w)`,
;;; so R was two states, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE`, its `--dump-answer` state
;;; files, and `ein oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s,
;;; release and debug alike). Neither state file loaded: a fact line
;;; `(neq w a)` is a parse error. The engine now gives the answer by hand.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}")
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (neq w ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, so w and {?x} differ")
